From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 07:59:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 45/45] hottyterm: hotty surfaces paint off the terminal's lock

io-reader held renderer_state.mutex for the whole of each hotty-blitz
paint: 35-50 ms when plx opens a palette, during which a key in another
window of the process, or an echo beside a repainting surface, waited
for the lock.

A repaint now runs on a thread of its own (hotty-paint) with the lock let
go, and the stream parses on meanwhile. A surface's first frame still
paints on io-reader, in order, also with the lock let go. While a paint
runs, every other caller of hotty-blitz waits on a condition that lets
the lock go (Hotty.hostReady), and has the host before the painter's next
paint. The painter's frames and effects are recorded, then applied under
the lock, which it takes back on demand (lockDemand), ahead of the
stream's next batch. The IO thread's tick doesn't wait for a paint (it
writes the keys to the pty), and it sends the painter's replies, so the
painter never blocks on the mailbox.

The renderer waits for each paint, as it waited for the lock before, so
one frame shows the cells and the surfaces drawn for them. Without that,
a delta's own output presented a frame of its own ahead of its paint,
and an echo after it waited a refresh. At the end of a synchronized
update (mode 2026) the renderer waits past the paint under way, until
one covers the update, so the cells after it never show beside a
surface's pixels from before it (hotty SPEC 6.3). After 100 ms held it
gives up and the next hold waits a frame, so batches that end faster
than they paint can't stop the screen. An update begun since drops the
hold: its own end holds again. Without a paint thread, paints keep the
lock, as before.

Upstream files touched: renderer/State.zig (the hold flag),
renderer/generic.zig (updateFrame honours it), termio/stream_handler.zig
(an update's end reaches hotty) and termio/Termio.zig (threadExit stops
the paint thread; processOutputLocked looks the inspector up per byte,
since the lock can go mid-parse and the inspector close meanwhile). No
apprt code, so macOS gets the same.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
